    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where $ \\sigma_{z}(1) $ and $ \\sigma_{z}(2) $ are the phenotypic standard deviations of the two traits and $ \\sigma_{z}(1,2) $ is the covariance of traits 1 and 2 in the same individual. The relationship between the three types of correlations is fairly easily derived. Noting that covariances are additive (Chapter 3) and assuming that all covariance is due to additive genetic and special environmental effects, $ \\sigma_{z}(1,2) = \\sigma_{A}(1,2) + \\sigma_{E}(1,2) $, where",
